3    21   Abstract
4
5    22   A proof of concept study involving the on-line coupling of automatic dispersive liquid-liquid
6
7    23   microextraction (DLLME) to ICP OES with direct introduction and analysis of the organic
8
9    24   extract is herein reported for the first time. The flow-based analyzer features a Lab-In-Syringe
10
11   25   (LIS) setup with an integrated stirring system, a Meinhard® nebulizer in combination with a
12
13   26   heated single-pass spray chamber, and a rotary injection valve, used as on-line interface
14
15   27   between the microextraction system and the detection instrument. Air segmented flow was used
16
17   28   for delivery of a microliter fraction of the non-water miscible extraction solvent, 12 µL of
18
19   29   xylene, to the nebulizer. All sample preparative steps including magnetic stirring assisted
20
21   30   DLLME were carried out inside the syringe void volume as a size-adaptable yet sealed mixing
22
23   31   and extraction chamber. Determination of trace level concentrations of cadmium, copper, lead,
24
25   32   and   silver   as   model    analytes     has   been   demonstrated    by   microextraction    as
26
27   33   diethyldithiophosphate (DDTP) complexes. The automatic LIS-DLLME method features
28
29   34   quantitative metal extraction, even in troublesome sample matrices, such as seawater, salt, and
30
31   35   fruit juices, with relative recoveries within the range of 94-103%, 93-100% and 92-99%,
32
33
     36   respectively. Furthermore, no statistically significant differences at the 0.05 significance level
34
35
     37   were found between concentration values experimentally obtained and the certified values of
36
37
     38   two serum standard reference materials.
