7              102   In this paper, in-syringe DLLME is explored for the first time as a “front-end” versatile
8
9
               103   microextraction platform for ICP-based detection. Diethyldithiophosphate (DDTP) is used as a
10
11
               104   selective chelating reagent on the basis of its ability of complexing metal species at the usual
12
13
               105   acidic pH values for sample conservation34 as opposed to its carbamate counterparts, i.e. no
14
15
16             106   additional buffering of sample is needed, which, in turn, make the analytical method
17
18             107   straightforward (with no need of pH optimization) and less prone to blank contamination. As a
19
20             108   consequence of the high stability constants of the DDTP chelates, even in strong acidic
21
22             109   conditions, back-extraction methods with increasing of the acidity and/or the addition of
23
24             110   competing metal species are proven inappropriate for quantitative recovery of DDTP complexed
25
26             111   metals.35,36 To tackle this issue, we have exploited h-TISIS as a viable interface for the direct
27
28             112   injection of the metal containing organic extracts into the ICP system. With this interface,
29
30             113   organic matrices are permitted whereby analyte dilution in the back-extraction solution in
31
32             114   conventional liquid-phase microextraction approaches of trace metals is circumvented.
33
34             115   Cadmium, copper, lead, and silver were chosen as model analytes and analyzed in varied
35
36             116   environmental and food matrices.
