                                   EXECUTIVE SUMMARY

Savannah River National Laboratory researchers have performed 9 experiments with simulated waste and
13 experiments with radioactive waste to determine methane generation rates (MGR) applicable to
Savannah River Site waste at temperatures greater than or equal to 100 °C. Data from these experiments
was used to generate temperature-dependent expressions to conservatively account for methane generation
at elevated temperatures. Measured MGRs and the derived expressions are reported in units of standard
cubic feet per hour per gallon of solution, where standard conditions are 25 °C and 1 atm.

The following conclusions are made as a result of the work presented in this report:

    •   The only organic molecules in Concentration, Storage, and Transfer Facilities waste that are
        expected to produce significant amounts of methane are organosilicon (notably, methylsilicon
        species associated with antifoaming agents) and methylmercury.
    •   Higher degrees of oxidation in organosilicon antifoaming agents lead to higher methane generation
        reaction rates. Polydimethylsiloxane exhibited the highest generation rate, while
        trimethylsilylmethanol exhibited the lowest.
    •   The highest MGR observed from 2H-associated (i.e., not expected to achieve temperatures higher
        than 140 °C) radioactive waste testing was 2.05×10-5 ft3 h-1 gal-1. This was observed from a
        composited blend of Tanks 38 and 43 with added salt to artificially increase the boiling point.
        Actual 2H MGRs are likely to fall below this value.
    •   The highest MGR observed from 3H-associated (i.e., expected to achieve temperatures as high as
        170 °C) radioactive waste testing was 3.73×10-5 ft3 h-1 gal-1. This was observed from a composited
        blend of Tanks 30 and 32 with added salt to artificially increase the boiling point. Actual 3H MGRs
        are likely to fall below this value.
    •   Expressions that bound the MGR from waste at temperatures less than or equal to 152 °C and less
        than or equal to 170 °C have been generated and proposed herein.
    •   An alternate MGR expression that bounds methane production from methylmercury has also been
        generated and proposed herein.
    •   The model expressions proposed in this document are expected to bound MGR production in
        Concentration, Storage, and Transfer Facilities waste, but overpredict MGR by less than the
        expression currently employed. Therefore, the compensatory actions listed in U-ESS-G-00014,
        Rev. 2 are likely more than sufficient to alleviate concerns with methane generation.
    •   The lack of frequent Defense Waste Processing Facility recycle material transfers to the 3H
        evaporator system greatly decreases the risk that large concentrations of methylmercury
        (> 300 mg L-1) will exceed 140 °C.
    •   The production of other gases (e.g., N2, CO2, etc.) by chemical reaction or degassing is expected to
        inhibit the accumulation of methane in vapor spaces of process vessels.

The following recommendations are made as a result of the work described in this report:

    •   The expressions developed in this report should be employed to account for methane generation in
        Concentration, Storage, and Transfer Facilities waste streams as appropriate.
    •   Additional testing should be performed to better understand the methane generation rates expected
        from methylmercury in radioactive waste in order to credit lower generation.




