219:Coverage cells use `n=<count>` when `n_studies > 0` and `data gap` when `n_studies = 0` with `status: populated`. Numerical cells use `—` for data gaps. Key-sources cells cite the top two or three contributing sources using numbered-alias convention. Per-page tail percentile (formerly `p95 (ppb)` column) stays staff-side per CLAUDE.md Part 19; the `p95_ppb` field remains in machine-readable frontmatter but does not surface on the public page. This 5-column table is a sanctioned exception to the multi-column-tables-discouraged guidance in [[docs/writing-style]].
237:The `Methodology` section, near the top, states the rules that govern the rest of the page: speciation is non-substitutable; basis is preserved and labeled, never silently converted; non-detect handling preserves the source's own reporting convention; source pooling is avoided across LOQ, period, geography, and analytical-basis differences; row-fit is determined by author scope, not by re-derivation; evidence tiers and confidence ratings follow CLAUDE.md.
239:The `Literature Evidence Summary` is generator-managed inside `hmi-hmtc-evidence-summary` markers. Columns are literature-native: analyte, subcategory designation in plain language, reported concentration range across cited sources (means and medians plus highest reported value), detection rate where supported, applicable regulatory cap with jurisdiction and basis, source count, confidence rating, basis note. No internal codenames, no percentile arithmetic, no CSV paths.
241:The `Source Evidence Inventory` separates measured product concentrations from intake or exposure estimates. Shows what each source actually reports: metal, evidence scope, n, statistic type, reported values, units, max or p100 if directly reported, row-fit, brief evidence note. Do not add p10/p50/p90/p95 columns unless the source itself reports those.
247:The `Broad Product Context: Author-Scope Index` is generator-managed inside `hmi-broad-context-sources` markers. Catalogues sources whose author-stated scope is broader than this row.
253:Inline and table citations on product-category pages use numbered aliases `[[sources/<cite-key>|1]]`. A `Sources` block at the end maps each number to the full source page.
257:Every non-stub product-category page must carry these `## H2` headings. Stubs (no contributing sources in routing audit) are exempt.
262:4. `## Source Evidence Inventory` — what each source reports at the sample level; hand-curated.
263:5. `## Broad Product Context: Author-Scope Index` — generator-managed inside `hmi-broad-context-sources` markers.
265:7. `## Levers to reduce contamination` — practical interventions ordered by impact magnitude. Sourcing → agronomic → processing → formulation → testing-and-QC → packaging-and-storage. Each lever names magnitude with a cited source.
266:8. `## How standards math uses this page` — explanation that the row-standard percentile is an aggregate, not a per-source decoration.
268:10. `## Sources` — source legend table auto-generated by `tools/evidence/build-source-legend.mjs`.
288:source_refs: ["[[sources/fda2023-ctz-guidance]]"]
295:Cross-link to parallel rules in other jurisdictions. Never silently substitute or round regulatory values. Preserve exact agency-source values. If a value is ambiguous in the source, note the ambiguity rather than resolving unilaterally.
320:WikiBiome federation: use `wikibiome_crosswalk` slugs in frontmatter as stable anchors. Prefer mechanistic framing: exposure → microbiome change → functional consequence → disease process. Name taxa at genus or species level where the source supports; do not aggregate up to phylum unless the source did. Keep self-contained enough to lift without the rest of this wiki as context.
337:Each course has an outline page and module pages. Module pages include Learning objectives, Core content (drawn from wiki pages via links), Case studies (linked to `sources/` and `products/`), Quiz items, Further reading. Keep DRY: link to canonical wiki pages rather than duplicating.
